import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DNWyyI0w.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"FAQ","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"faq.md","filePath":"faq.md"}'),s={name:"faq.md"};function n(i,e,d,h,c,l){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="how-is-a-run-different-from-a-chat-session" tabindex="-1">How is a Run different from a chat session? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-is-a-run-different-from-a-chat-session" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How is a Run different from a chat session?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A chat session is one context that degrades as it compacts. A Run is durable: it has its own thread, its own frozen plan, a participant record per agent with a resumable session, one branch per coding participant, an append-only verification log, and a per-call usage ledger. It survives a server restart. See <a href="/docs/concepts/runs">Runs</a>.</p><h2 id="why-did-my-task-fail-verification-when-the-agent-said-it-was-done" tabindex="-1">Why did my task fail verification when the agent said it was done? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-did-my-task-fail-verification-when-the-agent-said-it-was-done" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why did my task fail verification when the agent said it was done?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Because &quot;done&quot; is not the agent&#39;s call. A task reaches <code>done</code> only on a passed (or explicitly skipped) <code>TaskVerification</code> from something other than the agent that did the work — a shell command judged by exit code, or an independent verifier pass. Read <code>verification.reason</code> and <code>failureKind</code>; the latter tells you whether it was <code>work_missing</code>, <code>semantic_failure</code>, <code>proof_missing</code>, <code>environment_failure</code>, or <code>provider_failure</code>. See <a href="/docs/concepts/plans-and-tasks">Plans &amp; Tasks</a>.</p><h2 id="my-check-passes-but-the-task-is-still-not-certified-why" tabindex="-1">My check passes but the task is still not certified. Why? <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-check-passes-but-the-task-is-still-not-certified-why" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My check passes but the task is still not certified. Why?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Only a <strong>trusted</strong> check can mark a criterion proven, and a check is trusted only if it came from a source the run did not author — project config or an operator. The plan author writes both the criteria and the checks, so a self-authored <code>{cmd: &quot;true&quot;}</code> would otherwise certify anything. An untrusted check can still <em>fail</em> a task; it just cannot pass one.</p><h2 id="should-i-use-guided-or-autonomous-mode" tabindex="-1">Should I use guided or autonomous mode? <a class="header-anchor" href="#should-i-use-guided-or-autonomous-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Should I use guided or autonomous mode?&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Guided</strong> for anything repeatable: the engine owns the dispatch loop, selects ready tasks from the frozen plan, and only advances on a passed verdict. <strong>Autonomous</strong> for genuinely exploratory work where re-planning mid-flight is the point. If you find yourself amending a guided plan repeatedly, the brief was the problem, not the mode.</p><h2 id="why-does-planversion-matter" tabindex="-1">Why does <code>planVersion</code> matter? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-does-planversion-matter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why does `planVersion` matter?&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>1</code> means the plan was set once and never amended — what a repeatable run should look like. Anything higher is drift, and <code>originalChecklist</code> lets you see exactly what changed. Without that pair, an amended plan is indistinguishable from a plan that was right the first time.</p><h2 id="where-does-memory-live-and-is-it-committed-to-my-repo" tabindex="-1">Where does memory live, and is it committed to my repo? <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-does-memory-live-and-is-it-committed-to-my-repo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where does memory live, and is it committed to my repo?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Memory lives in Saidi under <code>memory/</code>, as plain markdown. It is <strong>never</strong> committed to a client repo. Personal memory follows the human (<code>memory/members/&lt;userId&gt;/global.md</code>); project and agent memory is shared org-scoped workspace knowledge. Config lives in repos; memory lives here. See <a href="/docs/concepts/memory">Memory &amp; Conventions</a>.</p><h2 id="why-is-my-project-agent-asking-permission-for-every-change" tabindex="-1">Why is my project agent asking permission for every change? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-is-my-project-agent-asking-permission-for-every-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why is my project agent asking permission for every change?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>That is <code>approval: confirm</code>, the default. Change it in the project&#39;s memory-file frontmatter, or grant specific low-risk actions via auto-approve in Settings rather than switching the whole project to <code>auto</code>. See <a href="/docs/reference/project-policy">Project Policy</a>.</p><h2 id="can-saidi-merge-or-force-push" tabindex="-1">Can Saidi merge or force-push? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-saidi-merge-or-force-push" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can Saidi merge or force-push?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No. Under the default <code>branch: new</code> policy, work lands on a <code>saidi/*</code> branch and a PR is opened. The merge is yours.</p><h2 id="my-reflex-didn-t-fire" tabindex="-1">My reflex didn&#39;t fire. <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-reflex-didn-t-fire" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My reflex didn&#39;t fire.&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Check in this order: (1) did the event reach the bus at all — the recent-event feed shows everything with a <code>matched</code> count; (2) did it match — <code>matched: 0</code> means your <code>source</code>/<code>type</code> or match tree is wrong, and an invalid regex in <code>matches</code> fails closed; (3) was it skipped — the fire log records cooldown, dedup key, or <code>maxFires</code>; (4) is it <code>pending</code> on <code>requireApproval</code>; (5) does the reflex have a <code>lastError</code>. See <a href="/docs/guides/building-a-reflex">Building a Reflex</a>.</p><h2 id="what-is-the-changed-operator-comparing-against" tabindex="-1">What is the <code>changed</code> operator comparing against? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-the-changed-operator-comparing-against" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is the `changed` operator comparing against?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The last value <strong>that specific reflex</strong> saw for <strong>that specific field</strong>. It keeps a small per-reflex-per-field memory — the only stateful operator. A first sighting counts as changed.</p><h2 id="why-does-a-cost-read-as-zero" tabindex="-1">Why does a cost read as zero? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-does-a-cost-read-as-zero" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why does a cost read as zero?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two different reasons, and Saidi distinguishes them. <code>unpriced</code> on a usage entry means tokens were spent but the provider never reported a price (an aborted Anthropic call, or a custom endpoint with no rates configured). <code>unreported</code> means the call ran and not one request reported a token — those zeros are the absence of a measurement, not a measurement of zero. Neither is silently rendered as free.</p><h2 id="what-does-costshared-mean-on-a-message" tabindex="-1">What does <code>costShared</code> mean on a message? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-costshared-mean-on-a-message" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What does `costShared` mean on a message?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The provider bills a turn once and reports no per-message cost, so a multi-turn message can only be apportioned. The shares sum to exactly what the turn cost, but no single one was quoted by the provider — so the UI says so rather than presenting an estimate as an invoice.</p><h2 id="two-runs-are-touching-the-same-repo-is-that-safe" tabindex="-1">Two runs are touching the same repo. Is that safe? <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-runs-are-touching-the-same-repo-is-that-safe" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two runs are touching the same repo. Is that safe?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Yes — a repo lock serialises delegations against one checkout, and custom agents take an equivalent agent lock. Custom agents also get a per-run <code>workDir</code>, so concurrent runs never share a directory.</p><h2 id="what-happens-if-the-server-crashes-mid-delegation" tabindex="-1">What happens if the server crashes mid-delegation? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-happens-if-the-server-crashes-mid-delegation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What happens if the server crashes mid-delegation?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every executing delegation is written to <code>inFlights</code> before it starts and cleared when it settles, so recovery can tell you exactly what was killed — its kind, target, task, and start time. Work you had already approved is marked so PRIME can re-delegate with <code>approved: true</code> instead of re-asking.</p><h2 id="does-a-single-user-install-need-the-org-tenant-machinery" tabindex="-1">Does a single-user install need the org/tenant machinery? <a class="header-anchor" href="#does-a-single-user-install-need-the-org-tenant-machinery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Does a single-user install need the org/tenant machinery?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>It is already using it — single-owner is the degenerate case, with one org and one member. That is deliberate: a one-person workspace and a fifty-person one run the same code path rather than forking into two products. See <a href="/docs/reference/orgs-auth-permissions">Orgs, Auth &amp; Permissions</a>.</p><h2 id="an-mcp-server-shows-as-unavailable" tabindex="-1">An MCP server shows as unavailable. <a class="header-anchor" href="#an-mcp-server-shows-as-unavailable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;An MCP server shows as unavailable.&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Most likely it needs an interactive OAuth authorisation that has never been completed. A headless or scheduled run cannot run that flow. Authorise it from an interactive session first, then re-probe. See <a href="/docs/guides/marketplace">Installing from the Marketplace</a>.</p>',33)])])}const u=a(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
